  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nGenerally dry weather today through late this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...A strong surface high pressure \nsettles across the northeast, keeping conditions dry. A strong \nlow-pressure system in the central Atlantic stays well to our \nsoutheast Monday night through Thursday as a result. \n\nBreezy conditions today in the north, with some wind gusts up to\n25mph possible. A weak cold front will move through tonight \ninto Tuesday. However, with very limited low-level moisture, \nnot anticipating any rainfall along the frontal boundary. High \ntemperatures are expected to be generally in the 50s mid-week, \ncooling towards the 40s through the end of the week. Overnight \nlows drop into the 20s and low 30s mid week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nNor'easter could bring beach erosion to the coast late this\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cutoff low off the Mid Atlantic Coast Wednesday morning, lifts\nnortheast through Thursday morning, then either continues\nnortheast into The Maritimes or heads eastward Friday.\n\nThere is still quite a bit of uncertainty on the exact track,\nstrength and nature of this storm. If the stronger more\nnorthern solutions for this Nor'easter are correct, then there \ncould be some minor beach erosion along portions of the Downeast\ncoast around the time of high tide from Tuesday night into at \nleast Thursday. While predicted water levels will remain below \ncoastal flood benchmarks, there could be sufficient wave \nsetup/runup over multiple high tide cycles to cause some minor \nbeach erosion at more vulnerable locations along the coasts of \nHancock and Washington County. With the vast majority of \nsolutions with the Nor'easter not favoring a stronger and more \nnorthward track at this time, the current chance for even minor \nbeach erosion with this system is very low, at 10-20%.",
